The replication confirms the original fidelity gap pattern with two additional observations. First, the collapsed model achieves the \emph{lowest} absolute obs\_MSE at $\dt{=}1$ (9.96 vs.\ baseline 16.08), indicating that the trained $\dt$-embedding improves world model quality overall---the pathology is selective degradation of reward predictions at longer horizons, not general model failure. Second, freezing the $\dt$-embedding reduces the reward gap from $3.11\times$ to $1.96\times$, consistent with its role in preventing the co-adaptation loop while leaving residual multi-step reward prediction difficulty.

\paragraph{Value prediction analysis (exp\_064).}
To quantify the downstream effect of RSSM reward bias on critic estimates, we compare critic value predictions $V(s)$ against actual evaluation returns across three conditions (Table~\ref{tab:value_pred}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Critic value predictions vs.\ actual evaluation returns. The CIVO model's critic is well-calibrated to its $\lambda$-return targets ($V(s) \approx$ target) but both $V$ and targets are disconnected from reality (VE${=}3.03\times$).}
